From: hawkff <109485367+hawkff@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 12:15:11 +0000
Subject: Let the keyboard reach every remove button in ad blocking lists

On the Allowed sites, Custom filters and Custom filter lists pages, the
keyboard could not reach the remove buttons in order: the list moved a
selection over its rows, and as a focus target of its own it took Tab
and Shift+Tab focus and sent it to the row nearest to the previous
control. The remove buttons now take focus, and the list itself does
not, so Tab enters at the first row, moves row by row, and leaves the
list at either end.

License: GPL-2.0-or-later - https://spdx.org/licenses/GPL-2.0-or-later.html

---
 .../adblock/settings/AdblockCustomItemFragment.java       | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/chrome/browser/adblock/android/java/src/org/chromium/chrome/browser/adblock/settings/AdblockCustomItemFragment.java b/chrome/browser/adblock/android/java/src/org/chromium/chrome/browser/adblock/settings/AdblockCustomItemFragment.java
--- a/chrome/browser/adblock/android/java/src/org/chromium/chrome/browser/adblock/settings/AdblockCustomItemFragment.java
+++ b/chrome/browser/adblock/android/java/src/org/chromium/chrome/browser/adblock/settings/AdblockCustomItemFragment.java
@@ -91,6 +91,14 @@ public abstract class AdblockCustomItemFragment<T> extends PreferenceFragmentCom
         mAddButton = rootView.findViewById(R.id.fragment_adblock_custom_item_add_button);
         mAddButton.setContentDescription(getCustomItemAddButtonContentDescription());
         mListView = rootView.findViewById(R.id.fragment_adblock_custom_item_listview);
+        // Otherwise the list moves a selection over its rows and the keyboard reaches the remove
+        // button of one row only.
+        mListView.setItemsCanFocus(true);
+        // A focusable list takes Tab and Shift+Tab focus itself and then selects the row nearest
+        // to the previous focus, so focus would enter at a middle row and Shift+Tab could not
+        // leave at the top. Without it, focus enters at the first row's button; inside the list,
+        // the list moves it row by row, and at either end it leaves to the next control.
+        mListView.setFocusable(false);
         String addDisabledReason = getAddDisabledReason();
         if (addDisabledReason != null) {
             mItem.setHint(addDisabledReason);
